These configuration scripts can run pypilot as a standard service

To install to a debian system:

sudo apt-get install python-gps python-serial libpython-dev python-numpy python-scipy swig python-pil python-flask python-socketio python-pip python-pylirc wiringpi

First build RTIMULIB2:

git clone ...
cd RTIMULib2/Linux/python
sudo python setup.py install

If you are using orange pi:
git clone  https://github.com/xpertsavenue/WiringOP-Zero
cd WiringOP-Zero
sudo make install

Now for pypilot:
git clone https://github.com/pypilot/pypilot
cd pypilot
sudo python setup.py install


# Install service units for an existing non-root account (from this checkout):
sudo ./scripts/update_systemd_openplotter.sh "$(id -un)"

# With no argument, the installer uses the account invoking sudo.
# Existing unit files are preserved; User= overrides select the account for
# pypilot, pypilot_boatimu and pypilot_hat. Restart running services afterwards.
# Configuration belongs in that account's ~/.pypilot directory.
# This installs only systemd units, not the other etc/ examples. Device access
# and realtime scheduling permissions must be configured separately.

# Enable the required services:
sudo systemctl enable pypilot
sudo systemctl enable pypilot_hat
sudo systemctl enable pypilot_web

# Optional access point: configure hostapd and dnsmasq separately first.
sudo systemctl enable hostapd
sudo systemctl enable dnsmasq


# optional splash screen
cp lcd/ugfx/splash /usr/local/bin
